AWS shipped session policies for EKS Pod Identity 

🔴AWS EKS Pod Identity had a big issue before this

◾If a pod gets compromised in your cluster. The attacker now has the full IAM role attached to it.

◾That role had access to 10 S3 buckets. The pod only needed one. You just leaked data from 9 buckets you never intended to expose. This is called over-permissioning. 

◾It happens in almost every production cluster I have seen. Now with session policies you can fix this.

◾You attach an inline policy to the Pod Identity association. That policy says this pod can only touch this one bucket. 

◾Even if the underlying IAM role has broader access. The attacker gets nothing extra.

◾Think of it like a security guard with a master key. Session policy says you can only open room 4 today. The master key does not matter anymore.

◾One hard rule AWS enforces. Session policies can never expand permissions. 

◾They can only restrict. No engineer can accidentally grant more access than the role allows.

◾This is the principle of least privilege.
 Most teams talk about it. Very few actually implement it at the pod level.


----
If your team is still giving AWS Keys for pods to access AWS services, you are doing it wrong 🤯

🚫That’s risky and an EKS anti-pattern.

🔴You can use Amazon EKS Pod Identity, which helps in automatically handling the credentials :

◾Pod → Service Account → IAM Role → Temporary AWS Credentials

- No secrets in containers.
- No credential rotation headaches.
- Each pod gets least-privilege access to AWS resources.
